{
  "name": "Getting a Smooth Finish: How to Properly Mud Drywall",
  "step": [
    {
      "name": "Step 1",
      "text": "Achieving that perfectly smooth drywall finish takes a bit of finesse, but it's totally doable with the right steps. First things first, make sure your joints are pre-filled if there are any gaps. Then, it's all about embedding your tape. You'll want to apply a thin, even layer of wet joint compound to the seam, then firmly press your tape into it, ensuring there are no air bubbles underneath. After that, it’s about layering. You’ll typically apply 2-3 thin coats of joint compound, each progressively wider than the last. The key here is *thin* coats. Thicker coats take longer to dry and can lead to shrinkage or ridges. With each coat, use progressively wider knives to feather the edges out smoothly. This helps blend the mud seamlessly into the wall, preventing visible humps. Drying time is super important; you’ll want to let each coat dry thoroughly, often overnight, before applying the next or even thinking about sanding. Rushing this step is a recipe for frustration. Always check your work under angled light to spot any imperfections before moving on. If you're tackling a big project or just want to guarantee a professional-looking finish, don't hesitate to reach out. We can help ensure your drywall looks perfect!",
      "@type": "HowToStep",
      "position": 1
    }
  ],
